   104	# ''It crosses a device boundary.'' Its effect or its answerer stands on some device other than the one where the hand acts, or the fleet must read its outcome. An act whose effect and observers rest on one vessel leaves at most a receipt in that vessel's daemon bag.
   105	# ''It settles.'' A hand committed it: save-and-publish, ask, approve, join, re-key, move a carrier's residency, grant, revoke. A keystroke, a cursor, a selection, a mid-edit draft, a cached invariant tiddler and every `$:/temp`, `$:/state` and `$:/status` title never qualify; they live in working memory and the volatile tiers.
   106	# ''It acts on content; it never carries content.'' Content travels on its own bag's document and its own sync. The plane names a carrier by its address or CID and says what to do with it; a body never rides an entry.
   107	# ''It expects an answer.'' An intent addresses someone and awaits an outcome. A broadcast of state that nobody answers belongs on a plane or document of its own, never here.
